WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.180
Steven Seagal was one of the biggest action stars&nbsp;
of the 90s — so where's he been lately? From his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.080
weird bromance with a foreign dictator&nbsp;
to his brief foray as a spy novelist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.226
here's what the Above the Law star has been up to.

00:00:12.853 --> 00:00:14.460
Starting in the late '80s, Steven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.320
Seagal took a side job as a police consultant in&nbsp;
Jefferson Parish, Louisiana. Sheriff Harry Lee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.360
a big Seagal fan, asked the action star to train&nbsp;
his force in martial arts and marksmanship. Seagal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:28.920
loved the job, and did it for so long that Lee&nbsp;
eventually enlisted him as a reserve deputy.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:34.380
In 2009, Seagal's side gig became the basis&nbsp;
for a reality show on A&amp;E called Steven Seagal:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.160
Lawman. It was similar to Cops in that&nbsp;
a film crew tagged along while police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.640
officers investigated local crimes&nbsp;
as they happened. The big difference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.220
obviously, was that the star&nbsp;
of Under Siege was there.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.240
"But no matter how great our deputies are&nbsp;
there's always going to be areas that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.536
problematic. And we're trying to get those areas."

00:00:50.536 --> 00:00:55.200
Sadly, Seagal reportedly resigned from the&nbsp;
force in 2010 after he became the subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.734
of an internal affairs investigation.&nbsp;
According to the Phoenix New Times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.734 --> 00:01:03.420
the investigation sought to uncover the truth&nbsp;
about "allegations of sex trafficking and sexual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.100
assault." Those allegations were corroborated by&nbsp;
two other female ex-employees who alleged similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.518
treatment by the actor. However, their suit&nbsp;
against Seagal was dropped later in the year.

00:01:12.518 --> 00:01:14.100
You may be thinking that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.180
Steven Seagal is very well suited for a&nbsp;
role in a scripted cop series. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.380
TV shows about the police are always pretty&nbsp;
popular, and Seagal spent years beating up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.680
bad guys in movies. Well, you'd be&nbsp;
right because it actually happened.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.380
In 2011, Seagal took his crime-fighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.360
butt-kicking act to TV with the action series True&nbsp;
Justice. In addition to creating True Justice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.020
Seagal played Elijah Kane on the series,&nbsp;
the head of an elite undercover Seattle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.340
task force. The show first aired in Spain&nbsp;
on a cable channel called Nitro before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.797
showing up on the American movie network&nbsp;
Reelz. True Justice ran for two seasons.

00:01:45.797 --> 00:01:48.360
In October 2017, Inside Edition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:53.280
correspondent Lisa Guerrero told The Hollywood&nbsp;
Reporter about her 1996 audition for Seagal's Fire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.020
Down Below. According to Guerrero, Seagal summoned&nbsp;
her to his home for a "private rehearsal" after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:03.060
the audition. Guerrero declined, and the lead role&nbsp;
she said she was promised went to another actor.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.740
"If you're an actress in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:10.080
you've been Harvey Weinsteined. In my case,&nbsp;
I was Harvey Weinsteined by Steven Seagal."

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.720
Television personality Jenny&nbsp;
McCarthy also spoke out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.500
saying Seagal demanded she take off her&nbsp;
dress during an audition for Under Siege&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.280
2. According to Entertainment Weekly,&nbsp;
Seagal denied McCarthy's allegations.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:25.080
In January 2018, Rachel Grant, a former&nbsp;
"Bond girl" who appeared in the 007 movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.400
Die Another Day, went public with her own&nbsp;
story about Seagal's alleged abuse. Grant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.600
told the BBC that during her audition&nbsp;
for Seagal's 2003 film Out for a Kill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.520
he pushed her onto a bed and pulled down his&nbsp;
pants. Again, Seagal denied any wrongdoing.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:43.200
Additionally, in January 2018, two women&nbsp;
filed police reports in Los Angeles that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.980
alleged Seagal had sexually assaulted&nbsp;
them. One of the women was Regina Simons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:51.600
who appeared as an extra in Seagal's 1994&nbsp;
action film On Deadly Ground. In her report,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.080
Simons offered a detailed account of&nbsp;
Seagal sexually assaulting her when she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:00.960
was 18. When reached for comment, the LAPD cited&nbsp;
confidentiality laws. However, the department did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.920
reveal that they were investigating yet another&nbsp;
claim against Seagal, according to The Wrap.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.700
After the multiple allegations surfaced,&nbsp;
it quickly became apparent that Seagal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.300
would have to do more than just have his&nbsp;
publicist deny the allegations. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.660
actor went on the offensive. While&nbsp;
appearing on Alex Jones' InfoWars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:20.040
Seagal told the host he was the victim of&nbsp;
a vast, well-funded conspiracy. The actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:24.600
also added that "40 percent" of the sexual&nbsp;
assault allegations coming out of the #MeToo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.764
movement were made up. Seagal also vowed&nbsp;
revenge on the people he said were lying.

00:03:28.764 --> 00:03:31.020
Steven Seagal is reportedly good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:34.680
friends with fellow martial arts movie&nbsp;
star Jackie Chan. So while Rush Hour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.400
3 was entering production in 2007,&nbsp;
Chan was rumored to have suggested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.000
that Seagal play the film's villain.&nbsp;
However, he didn't end up in the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.200
and the script was ultimately rewritten to&nbsp;
accommodate Japanese actor Hiroyuki Sanada.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:50.880
Seagal missed another opportunity for a huge&nbsp;
comeback when he passed on a role in Sylvester&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.620
Stallone's 2010 hit, The Expendables. The film&nbsp;
showed that there was still a big audience for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:00.300
'80s-style action movies, as well as '80s-style&nbsp;
action heroes. The cast of The Expendables series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.894
included Stallone himself, Chuck Norris,&nbsp;
and Jean-Claude Van Damme.

00:04:03.894 --> 00:04:07.620
Missing from that list, however, was&nbsp;
Steven Seagal. Seagal ultimately declined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:12.000
Stallone's offer because he hated one of the&nbsp;
movie's producers, Avi Lerner. The Under Siege&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.107
star and Lerner had previously worked on&nbsp;
several direct-to-video features together.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.980
Steven Seagal lives a very full life outside&nbsp;
of making movies. He also spends a lot of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:23.220
traveling around the world visiting his&nbsp;
friends who live abroad. As it happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.520
two of his best friends are Buddhist&nbsp;
spiritual leader the Dalai Lama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:31.380
and Russian president Vladimir Putin. In addition&nbsp;
to his international friends, Seagal gained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:35.880
citizenship in Serbia in January 2016 when he&nbsp;
offered to set up a martial arts studio there.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.540
Seagal's bromance with Putin has earned&nbsp;
the star some enemies. When he told the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:43.380
Russian state-run newspaper Rossiskaya&nbsp;
Gazeta that he considers Putin "one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:46.680
the great living world leaders," and that he&nbsp;
thinks of the Russian leader like a brother.

00:04:46.844 --> 00:04:50.804
"He is one of the greatest world leaders and&nbsp;
one the greatest presidents in the world."

00:04:50.804 --> 00:04:55.800
Furthermore, after Putin personally granted Seagal&nbsp;
a Russian passport in 2016, thus formally making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:59.580
him a citizen of the country, the actor&nbsp;
was banned from entering Ukraine. Writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:03.000
in a letter that was published on the news&nbsp;
site Apostrophe, Ukrainian officials said:

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.900
"[Steven Seagal has] committed socially&nbsp;
dangerous actions [that] contradict the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.935
interests of maintaining Ukraine's security."

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:13.980
Two Steven Seagal films — The Foreigner and&nbsp;
Contract to Kill — earned the not-so-prestigious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:18.660
score of 0 percent on Rotten Tomatoes. That&nbsp;
means that every critic who saw both films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:23.100
didn't like them. Regarding The Foreigner, Scott&nbsp;
Weinberg of eFilmCritic said in his review:

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.820
"[This movie is] so bad that it&nbsp;
makes Seagal's other films look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.460
like the combined efforts of&nbsp;
Frank Capra and Billy Wilder."

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:32.880
As for Contract to Kill, a&nbsp;
critic for The A.V. Club wrote:

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.880
"[Steven Seagal] gives the kind of&nbsp;
performance traditionally associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.840
with stars who died during filming. And&nbsp;
yet, Seagal is in almost every scene."

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.289
"Who. Is. The target?"

00:05:42.289 --> 00:05:46.020
So what do the critics think about the rest of&nbsp;
Seagal's recent, direct-to-video filmography?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:51.000
Well, those movies haven't even been seen&nbsp;
by enough people to be rated yet. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:56.160
as of May 2023, a whopping 53 of his&nbsp;
movies since 2001 are listed as "No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.300
Score Yet" on Rotten Tomatoes. Despite the&nbsp;
action star's incredibly long filmography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.195
you can count on one hand the number&nbsp;
of "Certified Fresh" movies he has.

00:06:04.195 --> 00:06:06.689
"I'm just a lowly, lowly cook."

00:06:06.689 --> 00:06:08.940
If you believe there's a secret globalist agenda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.660
that threatens to destroy the United States,&nbsp;
what's the best way of getting your message out?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:17.880
Well, since this is exactly what Steven Seagal&nbsp;
believes, his answer was to write an action-spy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:21.660
novel titled, The Way of the Shadow Wolves:&nbsp;
The Deep State and the Hijacking of America.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:27.060
Co-credited to Seagal and writer Tom Morrissey,&nbsp;
the novel is billed as "a book of fiction based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:31.860
on reality." The plot follows an Arizona tribal&nbsp;
officer who untangles a high-reaching conspiracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:35.160
among drug smugglers, the mainstream&nbsp;
media, and the United States government.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:40.620
As with many of Seagal's films, the reviews&nbsp;
weren't exactly kind. In December 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:45.180
The A.V. Club highlighted a series of Amazon&nbsp;
writeups and social media posts that gleefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:49.680
mocked the book and its authors. Furthermore,&nbsp;
A.V. Club critic Nathan Rabin "hate-read" the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:53.640
entire book in order to offer an in-depth&nbsp;
rundown of the story. On the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:58.283
the right-wing classic rocker Ted Nugent&nbsp;
posted on Facebook that he loved Seagal's book.

00:06:58.283 --> 00:07:01.680
In September 2017, Seagal appeared on Good Morning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:06.300
Britain to weigh in on the national anthem. At&nbsp;
the time, a large number of professional athletes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:11.040
were protesting racial injustice and inequality in&nbsp;
America by opting not to stand during the anthem.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.320
"We have a lot of people that aren't treated&nbsp;
equally, aren't given equal opportunities."

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.400
It's a complicated issue that's inspired&nbsp;
no shortage of debate, but from Seagal's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:24.360
point of view, it's all very simple. While&nbsp;
insisting that he believed in free speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:27.660
Seagal made it clear that he was&nbsp;
deeply offended by the idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:30.960
anyone would refuse to stand during&nbsp;
the anthem. He told host Piers Morgan:

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.920
"I don't agree they should hold the United&nbsp;
States of America and the world hostage by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:38.640
picking a venue where people are tuning in&nbsp;
to watch a football game and imposing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:42.936
political views. I think it's outrageous,&nbsp;
I think it's a joke. It's disgusting."

00:07:43.134 --> 00:07:45.198
According to multiple actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.198 --> 00:07:49.920
Steven Seagal is an incredibly difficult person&nbsp;
to work with. John Leguizamo, who co-starred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.480
with Seagal in 1996's Executive Decision,&nbsp;
discovered this the hard way. While filming the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:58.920
action-thriller, Seagal got angry with his co-star&nbsp;
and allegedly shoved Leguizamo against a wall.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:04.080
Seagal also made life hard for the Saturday&nbsp;
Night Live cast. While hosting the show in 1991,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:07.980
he was "very critical of the cast and writing&nbsp;
staff," according to Live from New York:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:11.520
The Complete, Uncensored History of&nbsp;
Saturday Night Live as Told by Its Stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.000
Writers, and Guests. Tim Meadows is&nbsp;
even quoted as saying in the book:

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.820
"[Steven Seagal] didn't realize&nbsp;
that you can't tell somebody they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:20.940
stupid on Wednesday and expect them to&nbsp;
continue writing for you on Saturday."

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:25.800
David Spade, meanwhile, said that in his six&nbsp;
years on Saturday Night Live, Seagal was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:31.080
absolute worst host, which probably had a lot to&nbsp;
do with his taste in comedy. For instance, former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:36.060
cast-member Julia Sweeney noted in Live from New&nbsp;
York that Seagal had an idea for a sketch in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:41.160
he played a therapist who attempts to sleep with&nbsp;
a rape survivor. As it turned out, Seagal was so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:44.880
impossible to work with that he was banned&nbsp;
from ever hosting Saturday Night Live again.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.860
Stephen Tobolowsky also learned firsthand about&nbsp;
Seagal's difficulty on set. In The Glimmer Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:53.940
Tobolowsky played a serial killer. So,&nbsp;
naturally, Seagal was supposed to blow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:58.860
the bad guy away. But then, as Tobolowsky told&nbsp;
The A.V. Club, Seagal decided it was "bad for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:03.240
his karma" to keep killing people on-screen.&nbsp;
So he wanted Tobolowsky's character to survive.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.000
"This is god's house, don't make me do this."

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.660
Thinking on his feet, Tobolowsky quickly&nbsp;
explained that his character was trapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:13.380
in his own private hell. So by killing&nbsp;
the villain, Seagal would be allowing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.911
bad guy to reincarnate as a more peaceful being.&nbsp;
Seagal agreed, and the scene went on as scripted.

00:09:17.911 --> 00:09:20.460
In addition to acting, Steven Seagal has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:25.200
done quite a bit of fight choreography. According&nbsp;
to IMDb, most of the films he's choreographed are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:29.400
his own. In the early '80s, however, Seagal&nbsp;
worked on the James Bond film Never Say Never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:34.440
Again. Apparently, he was brought in to teach Sean&nbsp;
Connery about martial arts. Connery, however, was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:40.020
already well-versed in karate. While preparing for&nbsp;
1967's You Only Live Twice, he was even awarded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:44.820
an honorary third-degree black belt. But for his&nbsp;
seventh outing as Bond, Connery needed to learn a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:48.900
little aikido, so Seagal was hired to teach him&nbsp;
how to throw opponents and manipulate joints.

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:52.080
At first, the lessons seemed&nbsp;
to be going well. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:57.060
they were going too well. As Connery admitted&nbsp;
to Jay Leno on The Tonight Show in 1996,

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:59.640
"And then I got a little cocky because&nbsp;
I thought I knew what I was doing [...]"

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.340
But then things took a turn&nbsp;
for the worse. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:05.700
an irritated Seagal allegedly&nbsp;
grabbed Connery's arm and broke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:10.980
the actor's wrist. While he was undoubtedly&nbsp;
in pain, Connery kept on training. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:14.940
he didn't realize that Seagal had actually&nbsp;
snapped his wrist until the late '90s.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:18.900
Of course, Seagal can take a bit of pain as&nbsp;
well. While working on his breakout film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:22.800
1988's Above the Law, actor Henry&nbsp;
Silva broke Seagal's nose with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:26.760
overzealous punch. Knowing the show must&nbsp;
go on, Seagal stayed up late that night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:30.134
icing his battered nose so he could&nbsp;
keep on shooting the next day.

00:10:30.134 --> 00:10:31.740
Steven Seagal has battled all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:35.580
sorts of bad guys on the silver screen,&nbsp;
from terrorists to the yakuza. But he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:39.840
also faced some scary villains in real&nbsp;
life, namely the mafia. In the '90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:44.820
Seagal teamed up with producer Julius R. Nasso.&nbsp;
Together, the duo made a string of hits. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:48.180
their relationship soured in 2000,&nbsp;
and the two eventually had a falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:51.780
out. But things took a dark turn when&nbsp;
the Gambino crime family got involved.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:56.160
One day, Seagal was ordered into a car and&nbsp;
escorted to a Brooklyn restaurant. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:00.780
there that he met an alleged Gambino captain named&nbsp;
Anthony "Sonny" Ciccone. According to CBS News,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:04.800
Ciccone ordered Seagal to start working&nbsp;
with Nasso again. He also demanded that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:11.040
Seagal fork over $150,000 for every film&nbsp;
he made. Evidently, Seagal was so shaken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:16.500
he gave the gangsters $700,000. The martial&nbsp;
artist had every reason to be afraid. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:20.700
as CBS News reported, as Seagal left the&nbsp;
meeting, someone supposedly told him:

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:23.580
"If you would have said the wrong&nbsp;
thing, they would have killed you."

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:27.540
The scandal came to light in 2003, when&nbsp;
the government indicted alleged crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:31.440
boss Peter Gotti and 16 others for&nbsp;
a whole host of crimes. In addition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:34.860
to Seagal's testimony, the government&nbsp;
had recordings of several individuals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:39.120
including Ciccone and Nasso. In the&nbsp;
recordings, the two men discussed how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:42.900
they would intimidate Seagal. They even laughed&nbsp;
about how they had scared the movie star.

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:47.760
Nasso defended himself by saying Seagal had backed&nbsp;
out of several movie deals, and owed him $500,000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:53.820
Regardless, the producer was sentenced to one year&nbsp;
in federal prison. But once a crooked mob guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:59.760
always a crooked mob guy. Nasso wanted his&nbsp;
cash, and sued Seagal for $60 million. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:03.818
the producer got out of jail, he settled with&nbsp;
Seagal for an undisclosed amount of money.

00:12:03.818 --> 00:12:06.000
While never a critical darling, Steven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.400
Seagal used to pull in some impressive box office&nbsp;
numbers. But throughout the 2010s, the majority of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:16.440
Seagal's films went straight to DVD or streaming.&nbsp;
Furthermore, Seagal isn't making as many movies as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:22.680
he used to. In fact, as of April 2023, Seagal's&nbsp;
last film, Beyond the Law, was released in 2019.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:27.660
So what happened to Steven Seagal's career?&nbsp;
Well, he blames his decline on the FBI.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:33.420
In 2002, Los Angeles Times reporter Anita Busch&nbsp;
was investigating Seagal about Julius Nasso and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:37.800
the producer's association with the mafia. But&nbsp;
one morning, she found a dead fish, a rose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:42.240
and a sign that read "Stop!" on her windshield.&nbsp;
There was also a brand-new bullet hole in her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:48.360
car. Afraid, Busch informed the FBI. As a result,&nbsp;
the feds began investigating Seagal. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:53.160
the FBI thought the actor had hired a detective&nbsp;
named Anthony Pellicano to intimidate Busch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:57.600
Pellicano was a private eye who would later be&nbsp;
convicted of illegally spying on celebrities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:03.180
The FBI also suspected Seagal of hiring a thug&nbsp;
to scare Vanity Fair writer Ned Zeman with a gun.

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:04.500
"I know you, don't I?"

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:09.180
But after digging deeper, the FBI concluded&nbsp;
there was no evidence against Seagal. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:14.400
they focused on Pellicano. And then, Seagal just&nbsp;
fell by the wayside. The FBI never charged him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:18.120
but they didn't exonerate him either.&nbsp;
According to the Los Angeles Times story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:22.920
the actor believes all the notoriety from the&nbsp;
Pellicano case majorly harmed his career — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:26.700
he may have a point. As one Hollywood&nbsp;
publicist told the Los Angeles Times:

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:30.300
"Steven Seagal was no Harrison Ford&nbsp;
when this [scandal] happened. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:32.520
these accusations certainly hastened his decline."

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:36.660
So it only makes sense that Seagal wants&nbsp;
an apology from the FBI for possibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:40.740
destroying his career. As for Pellicano,&nbsp;
he was convicted on wiretapping charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:45.637
in 2008 and sent to prison. Having served his&nbsp;
time, Pellicano was released in March of 2019.

00:13:45.637 --> 00:13:47.940
Steven Seagal's hands aren't just for breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:53.880
people's bodies — they're also for making sweet,&nbsp;
sweet music. Yes, the star of Hard to Kill is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:58.320
a long-time musician who loves the blues. Whether&nbsp;
the blues loves him back is another question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:03.720
entirely. Seagal has been playing guitar since&nbsp;
age 12 and heads the Steven Seagal Blues Band. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:08.820
wasn't until 2005, however, that Seagal released&nbsp;
his first album, Songs From the Crystal Cave.

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:12.720
The album features straightforward blues,&nbsp;
though the songs aren't nearly as grimy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:16.320
and soulful as something you might here&nbsp;
form B.B. King or Buddy Guy. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:20.940
SputnikMusic called the album "the 'Plan&nbsp;
9 From Outer Space' of records." There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:24.120
various reasons for the album's harsh&nbsp;
criticism, such as Seagal's mumbling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:28.200
half-asleep singing style. The actor's less&nbsp;
than impressive guitar-playing also didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:33.300
wow the critics. Additionally, the album featured&nbsp;
some downright bizarre lyrics, such as this gem:
 &nbsp;

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:38.019
"You're like a ghost / The more you eat,&nbsp;
the more you're hungry / A hungrier ghost."

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:43.140
He's got Russian citizenship, a willingness&nbsp;
to offer his many opinions on a wide variety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:47.700
of subjects, and a measure of celebrity.&nbsp;
So, it was probably only a matter of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:51.900
before the Russian government offered Steven&nbsp;
Seagal a job. That time came in August 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:56.580
when Seagal was made a "special envoy for&nbsp;
humanitarian ties" with the United States.

00:14:56.580 --> 00:15:00.120
That's a fancy-sounding gig, but as&nbsp;
the Russian Foreign Ministry noted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:03.720
when announcing Seagal's appointment,&nbsp;
it's an unpaid position. It's also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:06.840
position that's a little difficult to&nbsp;
understand, at least in terms of Seagal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:10.860
actually being able to get anything done. As&nbsp;
the Ministry's description of the role notes:

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:15.180
"[The envoy's job is to] facilitate relations&nbsp;
between Russia and the United States in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:19.860
humanitarian field, including cooperation in&nbsp;
culture, arts, public and youth exchanges."

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:24.060
One of 2022's most original and&nbsp;
critically adored films was The Menu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:28.620
a black comedy served with a zest of social&nbsp;
commentary and a dash of horror. Among other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:32.220
big names who appeared in the film,&nbsp;
such as Ralph Fiennes, Anya Taylor-Joy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:36.720
and Nicholas Hoult, the movie featured John&nbsp;
Leguizamo as a movie star named George Diaz.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:39.840
In the film, the Diaz character is&nbsp;
no longer relevant as a movie star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:43.560
instead fading into obscurity as he&nbsp;
becomes an ever-increasing target of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:48.600
ridicule. For inspiration in playing this washed&nbsp;
up has-been, Leguizamo chose his former co-star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:52.620
Steven Seagal. During an interview with&nbsp;
Entertainment Weekly, Leguizamo certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:56.868
didn't mince words when revealing where his The&nbsp;
Menu character came from. He told the outlet:

00:15:56.868 --> 00:16:00.000
"I kind of based [my performance]&nbsp;
on Steven Seagal. I had a bad run-in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.980
with him. [...] He's kind of a horrible human."

00:16:02.253 --> 00:16:04.293
"What are you blind? We're closed, okay?"

00:16:04.293 --> 00:16:06.000
"You kind of talk too much."

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:09.305
Since Seagal's position with the&nbsp;
Russian government is mostly honorary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.305 --> 00:16:12.960
it doesn't necessarily signify anything&nbsp;
about the actor's politics. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:17.100
it's harder to say the same about Seagal's&nbsp;
decision to hold his 70th birthday party in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:22.380
Moscow in April of 2022. After all, in attendance&nbsp;
at the bash were two pro-Putin journalists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:26.220
both of whom had already been sanctioned by the&nbsp;
European Union due to Russia's controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:30.660
invasion of Ukraine. Seeing as Putin's invasion&nbsp;
of Ukraine occurred only a few months prior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:34.620
in February of 2022, Seagal raised&nbsp;
eyebrows when he told his guests:

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:38.220
"I love all of you and we stand together,&nbsp;
through thick and through thin."

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:41.280
Putin is not Seagal's only&nbsp;
controversial friend in politics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:44.460
however. The actor has publicly voiced&nbsp;
support for former Philippine President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:48.420
Rodrigo Duterte, a leader whose&nbsp;
strict stance on crime earned him the nickname&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:52.380
"The Punisher." Seagal has also demonstrated&nbsp;
his support for Venezuelan President Nicolas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:55.920
Maduro, a leader whose&nbsp;
divisive tactics have led many news outlets to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:59.580
either question his status as a democratic&nbsp;
leader or outright pronounce him a dictator.

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:01.414
"You're hanging around with the wrong people."

00:17:01.414 --> 00:17:02.820
One of the great gifts of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:06.360
a famous actor is the ability to diversify&nbsp;
your income with celebrity endorsements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:11.160
and Steven Seagal hasn't missed the opportunity&nbsp;
to do just that. In 2018, the actor lent an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:15.600
endorsement to Bitcoiin2Gen, a cryptocurrency&nbsp;
aiming to rival Bitcoin. He also received a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:19.560
tidy sum for his support. However, Seagal&nbsp;
failed to disclose the payments he received,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:23.880
which violated U.S. federal law and led to charges&nbsp;
from the Securities and Exchange Commission.
&nbsp;

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:26.820
According to the SEC, Bitcoiin2Gen promised Seagal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:33.960
$250,000 in cash and $750,000 worth of B2G&nbsp;
tokens as payment for his promotions. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:39.780
actor ultimately received around $157,000. But&nbsp;
since he never reported his earnings, Seagal got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:44.520
in trouble. On its website, the SEC explained&nbsp;
the reasoning behind the charges, writing:

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:48.000
"These investors were entitled to know&nbsp;
about payments Seagal received or was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:51.120
promised to endorse this investment so they&nbsp;
could decide whether he may be biased."

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:54.000
Common sense also supports the SEC's reasoning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:57.360
as celebrities obviously advertise&nbsp;
products they don't necessarily support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:02.580
solely for the paycheck. To Seagal's credit,&nbsp;
upon learning of his offense, he cooperated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:08.160
with the SEC investigation, ultimately paying&nbsp;
around $314,000 in disgorgement and penalties.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:10.560
In addition to John Leguizamo and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:14.640
the cast of Saturday Night Live, you can also&nbsp;
add Charlize Theron to the list of celebrities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:19.260
who dislike Steven Seagal. During her June&nbsp;
22, 2020 appearance on The Howard Stern Show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:23.280
Theron made it abundantly clear that she&nbsp;
despises Seagal with a passion. When Stern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:27.240
asked Theron how she prepares herself mentally&nbsp;
for her roles in action films, the actress said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:30.540
that she often watches YouTube videos of&nbsp;
martial artists and their fights, adding,

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:33.600
"You always come across that odd Seagal&nbsp;
video of him 'fighting' in Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:38.280
but he really isn't ... He's overweight and&nbsp;
can barely fight. Look it up, it's ridiculous."

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:42.420
When Stern remarked on how comfortable Theron&nbsp;
seemed in publicly mocking another well-known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:44.580
industry professional, the actress told the host:

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:48.000
"[I have] no problem talking s--- about&nbsp;
[Seagal] because he's not very nice to women."

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:51.420
Though she didn't elaborate, Theron&nbsp;
was likely alluding to the multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:55.113
accusations of sexual assault and misconduct&nbsp;
leveled against Seagal over the years.

00:18:55.334 --> 00:18:57.300
It's no secret that Steven Seagal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.300 --> 00:19:01.620
is known for his international adventures, both on&nbsp;
and off the screen. As a friend of Vladimir Putin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:05.400
Seagal continues to meet with the Russian&nbsp;
President fairly regularly. But apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:09.240
becoming a Russian citizen wasn't enough&nbsp;
for the American actor. According to CNN,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:13.320
Putin honored Seagal in early 2023 with&nbsp;
an "Order of Friendship." As the news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:17.040
network noted, Putin cited the actor's&nbsp;
"great contribution to the development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:21.360
of international cultural and humanitarian&nbsp;
cooperation" as his reasoning for the honor.

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:25.320
The award itself is a relatively recent&nbsp;
invention, having first been instituted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:29.400
former President Boris Yeltsin in 1994.&nbsp;
Seagal's award comes off the heels of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:33.780
his 2022 visit to a destroyed Ukrainian prison&nbsp;
during the conflict between Russia and Ukraine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:37.500
Appearing on the government-owned network&nbsp;
Zvezda, Seagal surveyed the damages with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:41.700
the intent of educating those who haven't seen&nbsp;
the destruction first-hand. It should be noted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:44.940
however, that the actor did all of&nbsp;
this from a Kremlin-based perspective.

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:49.500
But what more can be said about Seagal's strange&nbsp;
ties to Putin and the Russian government? The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:53.100
actor has made some very interesting choices&nbsp;
in recent years — including his infamous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:57.360
birthday party in Moscow. He also consistently&nbsp;
finds himself honored by Putin and the Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.360 --> 00:20:01.800
government, so one can only wonder what position&nbsp;
or award Steven Seagal will be given next.

